# Deviations after freeze
Date, what changed, why, and whether results had been seen. Empty until the freeze.

- Nov 6, 2026, before freeze, disclosed for completeness: the pipeline dry run on pilot models computed feature files before the freeze, but no score values or comparisons were inspected; only ok or fail lines and timings were read.

- Nov 6, 2026, after freeze, before any confirmatory training: training bug fixed. Parameters were held in bf16, whose coarse precision rounds away or quantizes small Adam updates. They are now float32 with bf16 autocast, and saved as float32 so task vectors carry no rounding artifact. Found when reviewing pilot scores (task-vector cosine 0.87 across seeds, flat full-weight loss), so pilot scores had been seen; no decision rule, threshold or hypothesis changed. Earlier pilot models and timing used the bug and are superseded.

- Nov 6, 2026, after freeze, before any hub model was downloaded or scored: hub siblings are a seeded random sample of up to 800 usable derivatives per family, at most 3 per publisher (`src/sample_hub.py`), not all usable derivatives. Reason: scoring all 4,092 needs about 3 TB of downloads, and publishers limit effective sample size. Chosen from metadata only. Stratum coding covers the sample only.

- Nov 6, 2026, after freeze, before any confirmatory score: extraction precision fixed. `extract_blackbox.py` and `extract_whitebox.py` loaded weights as bf16, the rounding problem already fixed for training. Both now load float32. Adapter loads now use the pinned base revision, which `base_revision` manifests previously skipped. Found by reading the code, then measured on the pilot family only (`src/compare_precision.py`). No rule, threshold or hypothesis changed.

- Nov 6, 2026, after freeze, before any copy is trained: specification of details the preregistration left open for copies (`src/make_copies.py`, `src/transform.py`). Further tuning and student prompts use a dataset other than the claimant's (c0: oasst1; c1 and c2: ultrachat), as an attacker would not hold it. Merge partners are high-stratum siblings of the next claimant's dataset, so trained on other data. Quantization is simulated: per-tensor uniform on 2-D weights, stored dequantized. Pruning is per-tensor magnitude on linear layers, excluding embeddings and the output head. Students start from the shared base, 2,000 steps, full-weight at 2e-5, on responses sampled at temperature 0.7, top-p 0.9, up to 128 new tokens. Chosen with no scores computed.

- Nov 6, 2026, after freeze, before any hub model was downloaded or scored: stratum coding details. Identical cards with identical metadata are coded once and the code applies to all (`data/strata/groups.json`). Cards are cut to the first 3,000 characters plus up to 10 later lines mentioning data. `data_overlap` is coded for each claimant dataset. Both passes use the same model, claude-sonnet-5-5, on differently ordered batches (`data/strata/INSTRUCTIONS.md`). Coding is from cards and metadata only.

- Nov 6, 2026, before any hub model was downloaded or scored: stratum coding finished, and it departed from its instructions in two ways, found by auditing the coders' tool calls. (1) Several coding runs read cards through their own paging scripts, some cutting a card at 1,300 to 2,200 characters rather than the 3,000 supplied, so coverage was uneven. No script assigned a label; those flagged only checked that evidence quotes occur in the card. Four pass 2 runs listed filenames in the pass 1 directory; no run read the other pass's content. (2) The first two batches of each pass got no instruction that evidence must be one contiguous quote, so some evidence strings there are stitched from separate phrases. Effect (`src/audit_coding.py`, `data/strata/audit.txt`): all 40 cards naming a claimant dataset name it within the 3,000 characters supplied, and 36 within the first 1,300, so high and mid are little affected; up to 283 models coded all-unknown have their first data keyword only after character 1,300, an upper bound that includes template headings, so some low-stratum models may sit in unknown. Unknown is excluded from stratified references and kept in pooled ones. Codes were not edited after this audit, and no recoding was triggered: kappa on data_overlap is above the 0.6 rule (`data/strata/agreement.txt`). Hub strata remain secondary. As planned, named-dataset hub strata fall under the 19-member floor for rejection at alpha = 0.05 (ultrachat 5, oasst1 6, gsm8k 17 same or partial), so they are reported as unable to reject, and merged only under a rule fixed before scoring.

- Nov 6, 2026, the human's decision, before any hub model was downloaded or scored: hub siblings are limited to models tagged Apache-2.0 or MIT and models with no licence tag, for private research use with no redistribution. The 61 sampled models with other tags (non-commercial, "other", AGPL and similar) are excluded on metadata only, which leaves 1,539. Models with no tag grant no explicit rights, and the paper says so. Llama-family and Gemma-family derivatives are excluded from independent controls, to avoid accepting those terms. The human agreed to 8 bases; on checking, 2 of those (SmolLM2-135M and its Instruct derivative) belong to the pilot family, which never contributes to results, so they were removed. Four ungated, permissively licensed bases were added from a metadata-only census (`data/census_controls/`, Nov 6) to meet the preregistered 10: Qwen2-0.5B, Qwen2.5-0.5B, Qwen2.5-1.5B-Instruct, Phi-3-mini, Phi-2, Mistral-7B-Instruct-v0.2, Qwen3-1.7B, SmolLM3-3B, Granite 3.3 2B Instruct and GPT-2. Falcon3 and StableLM were considered and rejected because their base licence is tagged other. Controls are 11 per base, seeded, at most 2 per publisher (`src/make_download_list.py`). Downloads use no account token, so no gated repository can be reached. Repo code is never fetched, and safetensors are preferred over pickles. Repos above 40 GB are recorded and skipped.

- Nov 6, 2026, before any hub model was downloaded or scored: the weight check that the preregistration names but does not define. A hub sibling passes if at least 90% of the declared base's parameter names exist with matching shapes after merging any adapter, and the cosine between its flattened weights and the base's, over those parameters, exceeds 0.5. It fails if its weights are bit-identical to the base. The check uses the base only, never a claimant, and all failures are listed with their reason. Controls need no check.

- Nov 6, 2026, after freeze, before any confirmatory copy: quantization replaced. Uniform min-max quantization per tensor, specified earlier today, was found in the pilot to destroy 4-bit copies (about 0.1% top-1 agreement with the claimant), so they would not be usable copies. Now simulated from deployed formats and stored dequantized, on linear layers only: 8-bit symmetric with per-row scaling, and 4-bit NF4 with blocks of 64, using the table in the bitsandbytes source. Pilot agreement with the claimant was seen when this was decided, which is a departure to disclose, since H4(b) tests power on quantized copies. No sibling comparison or false-accusation rate entered the decision.

- Nov 6, 2026: timestamps. Both `.ots` proofs upgraded to Bitcoin attestations, earliest block 974838 at 18:44 UTC, before any confirmatory score. `ots verify` needs a local Bitcoin node, so each block's merkle root was compared with public headers from a block explorer (`prereg/timestamps/attestation_check.txt`). The timestamp covers the files named in the manifests. Entries in this file dated after it are not covered by that hash and are protected only by git history until a later manifest is stamped.

- Nov 6, 2026, after freeze, before any confirmatory score: audited methods aligned with their source text, read in `2410.14273`, `2509.25448` and `2609.31784`. **REEF:** the extractor used the last layer on SQuAD prefixes. It now uses linear CKA at the layer matching the paper's layer 18 of 0 to 31, on 1,000 TruthfulQA question-answer samples, the paper's dataset (Apache-2.0, ungated, prompts only; `src/build_reef.py`). Mean pooling over tokens is our choice, as the passages read do not state one. The paper states no accept threshold, so the preregistered 95th percentile of independent controls applies, as an original-style rule. **Witness Overlap:** pair-type rule of Eq. 6, Frobenius cosine, one witness, the smaller endpoint score as input to a logistic regressor. The threshold is learned on the other confirmatory family, with parent-child pairs (base, sibling) and sibling pairs (claimant, sibling). The pilot family is not used, in line with the rule that it never contributes to results. With two families this is a smaller training set than the paper's leave-one-family-out over 16. Roles are parameter names with the layer index removed; this is our reading of the paper's "blocks". A false accusation is a sibling pair typed as parent-child. **LLMPrint:** suffix 20 tokens, 1,000 GCG iterations, alpha 0.5, beta 1, 300 pairs from the paper's word list, threshold mean plus 1.64 standard deviations on 13 unrelated validation models. Gray-box verification only; black-box sampling at T = 100 is not run. GCG search width 512 and top-k 256 are the common defaults, since the paper says "default settings". The instruction template is applied through each model's chat template, our choice. All are reimplementations and are labelled so. Pilot models and timing were used for testing; no sibling comparison entered these choices.

- Nov 6, 2026, after freeze, before any confirmatory score: analysis code (`src/analyze.py`, `src/audit.py`) written and tested on synthetic data only (`src/test_analyze.py`). Three readings of the preregistration are fixed here. **S2:** "recipe family" is taken as the recipe cell (full at each learning rate, LoRA r=8, LoRA r=16), since the high stratum holds only one of full and LoRA by construction, so holding out full against LoRA is impossible within it. **S3:** run within the low stratum only, since all mid-stratum siblings of a claimant share one dataset by design. **H4(b):** "lowest-budget further fine-tuning" means 20 steps, for both full and LoRA, and "quantized" means both 8 and 4 bits; all four must reach power 0.80 for the same score. Controlled-sibling intervals resample dataset-recipe cells as preregistered, and there are few of them (about 12 per family), so those intervals are reported with their cluster count and read cautiously.

- Nov 8, 2026, before any confirmatory score: adapter loading fixed, and the weight check rerun on every hub sibling (`src/adapter_load.py`, `slurm/hub_check2.sbatch`, `outputs/hub/weight_check_v2.jsonl` on the cluster). The rule from Nov 6 is unchanged, and the check still uses the base only, never a claimant. The first run had two loader faults. (1) Adapters saved with a resized embedding could not load; the base is now resized to the adapter's vocabulary first. (2) Adapters saved with a repeated or shortened key prefix merged as nothing, without error, because PEFT dropped their weights, so they were wrongly recorded as identical to the base; keys are now normalized, and an adapter with nonzero weights that fails to apply raises an error. The same loader is used for all feature extraction. Failure causes were read from error text and files on disk, not from any score. Effect: passes rise from 1,256 to 1,296 of 1,539 (22 load failures and 18 false identicals recovered). Seven models recorded as low cosine are relabelled `nonfinite_weights`, since every weight tensor in them is non-finite; they still fail. During the rerun, one model that passed before failed because of a fault in the new embedding detection, which mistook a trainable-token delta for an embedding; that was fixed and the model rechecked. Left excluded after inspection, none being a loader fault: 33 repos with weights only in subfolders (the downloader takes the repo root only), 6 with empty or placeholder weight files, 5 with a config that does not match the checkpoint, 2 adapters whose own config declares a different base, repos in MLX, OpenVINO or quantized formats, unknown architectures, 16 needing custom code (never enabled), and adapters that are truly empty or all zero.

- Nov 8, 2026, before any LLMPrint score exists: LLMPrint is kept at the paper's full settings (300 pairs, 1,000 GCG iterations, 6 claimants), and no method is cut for cost. Float32 search was measured at about 430 GPU-hours, and packing processes on a GPU did not help. The GCG search now runs under bf16 autocast (`--autocast`), which cost 20 s per 100 iterations on Qwen3 and TinyLlama against 66 s and 107 s, with mean final search loss unchanged on the pilot family (0.7047 against 0.7045, 8 pairs) and in small timing runs on the confirmatory claimants (2 pairs each). The paper does not specify precision. Fingerprint bits are always read in float32 at verification. Two processes per GPU gave 28% more throughput than one, so the expected cost is about 80 GPU-hours. Search-stage measurements use claimants only and no suspect was scored.

- Nov 8, 2026, before any confirmatory score: specification of how each method's original rule is applied, which the preregistration left to the analysis code (`src/apply_rules.py`, tested on pilot features). **H1 and H5** use each method's own decision, not the rank test: Nikolic and MPS under pools P1 and P2; REEF at the 95th percentile of independent controls, per claimant; Witness Overlap as in the Nov 6 entry, with balanced class weights in the logistic regression (our choice); LLMPrint at mean plus 1.64 standard deviations over 13 controls drawn with seed 2027 and left out of the evaluation; keyed as one or more exact secret responses. Lineage and task-vector cosine have no same-architecture independent nulls here, so their original rules are reported as not applicable, as the preregistration provides, and these scores enter only the sibling-reference analyses. The rank test against independent controls in `analyze.py` is kept and reported as a separate grid. Holm correction runs across method by family, over the sibling and hub suspects together, with the kind and stratum breakdown reported alongside. Power intervals on copies resample claimants, since copies have no dataset-recipe cell, and with 3 claimants per family they are read cautiously. **MPS** permutations now run in batches in torch, on GPU, since the NumPy version would have needed tens of CPU-hours; the algorithm is unchanged, and synthetic checks (`src/test_rules.py`) show a nonempty set at 0.05 on equal candidates. **Keyed:** one keyed variant per claimant, continued from the claimant root with 20 secret pairs at a quarter of the training pool, full-weight, learning rate 2e-5, 2,000 steps. These settings ran once on two pilot claimants, recovering 100% and 85% of their own keys, with no change made afterwards. Keyed copies are not built, so H5 covers false accusation only, and the keyed method's power on copies is not measured.

- Nov 8, 2026, before any LLMPrint score exists and with no confirmatory score inspected: changes and departures found by reading the source papers in full text. **Changed:** LLMPrint declares a positive at bit agreement greater than or equal to the threshold, as in the paper's Algorithm 2; the code used strictly greater (`src/apply_rules.py`). **Departures kept and disclosed.** *Nikolic:* the paper states a one-sided z-test, its public code uses a two-sided test with Fisher's exact test for small counts, and ours follows the code. The code's default is 5,000 prompts, which we use, while the paper's headline results use 3,000 and it reports precision falling as prompts increase. Its controls are pretrained models; ours are derivatives of other bases. *MPS:* the paper's p-value has no added 1, ours does, which is slightly conservative and has no effect at alpha 0.05 with R = 1,000. We use 20,000 prompts as preregistered, against the paper's 100,000, and the paper reports performance reaching a plateau between 15,000 and 70,000 prompts depending on setting. *Thakur and Khoury:* our lineage score omits the trace-concentration gate of the paper's Eq. 9. Its original rule is not applicable here in any case, and the score enters only sibling-reference analyses. *LLMPrint:* the paper's 13 validation models are distinct pretrained families; ours are 13 seeded controls, which are derivatives of other bases. *REEF:* the paper's main experiments use 200 TruthfulQA samples, with an ablation up to 1,000; we use 1,000. Its main text states no pooling and no threshold, and those choices remain ours as logged on Nov 6.

- Nov 9, 2026, before any confirmatory score is computed and with no feature value read: replacement rule for independent controls that cannot be loaded. The preregistration requires at least 100 usable controls across at least 10 bases, and 110 were sampled. When this was written, 10 controls had failed to load, with one more expected to fail, which would leave at most 99. Causes, read from error text only: no usable weights or config at the repo root, adapters that cannot be applied, size mismatch, unsupported model type, and custom code, which is never enabled. **Rule:** each control base's set is the first 11 usable models in its seeded order. `src/make_topup_list.py` continues the permutation of `make_download_list.py` (`default_rng(2027)`) past the first 11 accepted models, with the same filters (full safetensors or adapter, not quantized, not gated, licence Apache-2.0, MIT or no tag, at most 2 per publisher, with lost originals still counting towards the cap), and it stops with an error unless its first 11 per base reproduce the frozen list. Reserves are fixed at the next 6 per base (60 in all), from the Nov 6 census metadata at its pinned commits, and go through the same downloader (`src/download_hub.py`). `src/topup_controls.py` walks the reserves in order and stops once a base has 11 usable models, so unused reserves are ignored and never scored. **Usable** means black-box features were written, the same test applied to the original controls. Lost controls are replaced rather than repaired, as hub siblings failing the weight check are excluded rather than repaired. A failed reserve, or one that cannot be downloaded, is passed over for the next in order, and no value of any feature enters the decision. If a base runs out of reserves it stays short and that is reported. The walk starts only after Qwen3 extraction ends, so the list of lost controls is final; failures depend on the model and not on the family, and `src/merge_topup.py` reports the usable count for each family separately. **Details.** Replacement features are computed once and written to both families' directories, since prompts are shared and controls have no family. Controls are not used by white-box scores. Accepted replacements are appended to each family's `manifest.json` once its extraction has finished, keeping the earlier file as `manifest_before_topup.json`. The LLMPrint validation draw (13 controls, seed 2027, `src/apply_rules.py`) is taken from the sorted usable controls, so it will differ from the draw over the unreplaced set; the code and seed are unchanged and it is made before any score exists. **State when written:** feature files exist for most models, and only their ok or fail status has been read. No `scores.csv`, `rules.jsonl` or `llmprint.jsonl` exists for either family.
